            {
                "entry_id": "5edcf01f-f778-3ca5-9b6e-a3cc1e43d6a7",
                "word": "Indic",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "78615500-23f0-3aad-88d6-cb1bc3f48642",
                        "entry_id": "5edcf01f-f778-3ca5-9b6e-a3cc1e43d6a7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A branch of the Indo-European family of languages comprising Sanskrit and its modern descendants such as Bengali, Hindi, Urdu, and Punjabi."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f3673d75-6d6f-4c32-910c-7ab1ad848a77",
                                "sense_id": "78615500-23f0-3aad-88d6-cb1bc3f48642",
                                "text": "Second, it is the only group that directly attests to a period of common development between two branches of the Indo-European family, namely, Indic and Iranian."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "50a6238d-7c30-4a9b-b202-879506a57232",
                                "sense_id": "78615500-23f0-3aad-88d6-cb1bc3f48642",
                                "text": "Similar transfers in Indic are fewer in number owing to the early loss of the aor.: for example, the pres. cayate 'notices' (aoristic root ci-, PIE *kʷey) is in origin the subj. of root aorist ácet, and displaces the earlier redup. Pres cikéṣi (AV), imperf. áciket (RV)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
